vm-compiler: two analyzer/codegen decline causes surfaced by the asdf.lisp
compile (v1.8.38's per-function decline report names them).

Compiling modules/asdf/asdf.lisp byte-compiles 586 functions; 3 decline:

1. "Unbound variable: &REST" (an ERROR escaping the analyzer, not a clean
   decline message):
     [218] ASDF/PARSE-DEFSYSTEM:PARSE-COMPONENT-FORM
     [243] ASDF/SOURCE-REGISTRY::PROCESS-SOURCE-REGISTRY-DIRECTIVE
   Something in these bodies makes the analyzer EVALUATE the symbol &REST as
   a variable — likely a DESTRUCTURING-BIND / macro lambda-list form the
   analyzer walks as ordinary code, or a (lambda (&rest) ...) shape it does
   not parse.  Repro: byte-compile asdf.lisp with :print t and look at the
   two functions; reduce from there.

2. "RETURN-FROM UIOP/RUN-PROGRAM::REDIRECT — no lexically visible BLOCK":
     [112] UIOP/RUN-PROGRAM::%REDIRECTED-SYSTEM-COMMAND
   A RETURN-FROM whose BLOCK the analyzer does not see — either a genuine
   macro-expansion artifact (the block is established by a macro the analyzer
   expanded differently than the interpreter would) or an analyzer scoping
   gap.  Determine which; if the code is genuinely conforming, fix the
   analyzer.

All three functions fall back to the interpreter (correct results, slower) —
these are compiler COVERAGE bugs, not correctness bugs.  The decline report
(:PRINT/:VERBOSE) is the repro harness.
